  - What tax consequences should I expect from proposed settlement options (lump sum vs periodic spousal support; sale of property)?
  - How do we identify and address any hidden assets or transfers by my ex-spouse?

- Support, custody, and children (if applicable)
  - What custody arrangement do you recommend as a starting point for negotiations, and how is child support calculated here?
  - Can temporary custody/support be put in place before final settlement/hearing?
  - How do settlement terms affect visitation, relocation, and enforcement if the other party moves away?

- Settlement terms & drafting
  - Can we include an explicit immigration carve-out (i.e., settlement does NOT waive immigration remedies or statements to immigration authorities)?
  - Should settlement include confidentiality clauses, and what should they cover/omit (e.g., not limiting reporting to government agencies)?
  - Do any release clauses waive rights that could affect me later (pension benefits, pension survivor rights, social security)?
  - What enforcement mechanisms and jurisdictions should we include if the ex-spouse refuses to comply (contempt, liens, wage garnishment)?
  - Should I accept lump-sum payments or ongoing support? What are the pros/cons for enforcement and tax reasons?
  - Are we protected if the other party files bankruptcy later?

- Litigation strategy & timing
  - Given our first hearing next month, what are the immediate steps you will take and what do you need from me before then?
  - Would mediation be recommended now, or should we push for formal discovery first?
  - What are the likely timelines and costs for settlement vs litigation?
  - What is your recommended negotiation strategy and my “bottom line” (what we will accept vs what we won’t)?

- Safety, communications & evidence
  - Should I stop or limit communication with my ex-spouse? Should communication be in writing only?
  - What evidence or documentation do you want preserved now (texts, emails, bank records, photos, witness names)?
  - If there’s any history of abuse, should I consider a restraining order, and will that affect immigration? (Ask Amy to coordinate with an immigration attorney)

Documents to bring / assemble before the meeting
- Identity & status:
  - Passport, visa, I-94, green card (front/back), naturalization certificate (if any), any USCIS receipt notices
  - Copies of any pending immigration filings and notices from immigration court
- Relationship & court:
  - Marriage certificate, divorce/ separation papers already filed, any prior court orders
- Financial (last 3–5 years where possible):
  - Tax returns (federal & state), recent pay stubs, W-2s/1099s
  - Bank and credit card statements (last 12–24 months)
  - Retirement account statements (401(k), IRA, pensions), stock/brokerage accounts
  - Mortgage statements, property deeds, vehicle titles/registrations
  - Business financials (profit/loss, balance sheet) if any business is involved
  - Debts: loans, credit cards, student loans
  - Insurance policies (life, health, homeowner/renter)
- Children:
  - Birth certificates, school records, childcare costs, health insurance info
- Evidence:
  - Relevant emails, text messages, social media posts, receipts, proof of separate property (if claiming separate)
- Agreements:
  - Prenuptial or postnuptial agreements, separation agreements, prior settlement offers

Settlement items to insist on or to avoid
- Insist on:
  - A clear immigration carve-out: explicit language that settlement does not waive or prejudice immigration claims/rights (have your attorney draft exact wording)
  - Specific enforcement provisions (how and where to enforce payment or property transfers)
  - Clear valuation methods and schedules for asset division (who pays what, when)
  - Protection for retirement accounts (QDRO language) and tax indemnities where appropriate
  - Survival clauses for necessary representations (e.g., tax filing obligations, undisclosed debts)
  - Temporary orders protecting your immediate financial needs before finalization
- Avoid:
  - Broad release language that waives future claims including immigration or criminal reporting
  - Relying solely on the other party’s promise without enforceable terms (e.g., informal transfer of title without court order)
  - Accepting oral agreements — get everything in writing and court-approved if possible
  - Quick sign-offs if you don’t fully understand tax, immigration, or enforcement consequences

Practical tips for the meeting and negotiation
- Prioritize the issues that immediately affect your immigration status and income/security.
- Bring a one-page timeline of events that changed your status (dates of filing, notices received, conditional permanent resident dates, etc.).
- Ask Amy to draft any immigration-protecting language and, if she’s not an immigration specialist, coordinate with an immigration attorney to review settlement drafts.
- Do not sign anything from your ex-spouse without Amy reviewing it first.
- Preserve all communications (don’t delete texts/emails) and consider taking screenshots with timestamps.
- Consider mediation for faster resolution, but only if the mediator and proposed terms won’t harm your immigration options.

Sample short immigration-protection clause (example — have Amy or another attorney tailor/draft)
- “Notwithstanding any other term in this Agreement or any release herein, nothing in this Agreement shall be construed to waive, prejudice, limit, or affect [Your Name]’s rights to pursue any immigration relief, to make truthful statements to immigration or law enforcement authorities, or to apply for any immigration benefit. Any provision inconsistent with this shall be void as to immigration matters.”

Next steps I suggest
