I can help. Below is a focused checklist of questions and topics to raise with Amy Foster (and with an immigration attorney if Amy isn’t handling immigration as well), plus a short list of documents to bring and red flags to watch for. I’m not a lawyer — treat this as a practical planning checklist to use with your counsel.

Top (must-ask-before-any-settlement) questions
- Are you representing me on both the divorce and immigration issues, or should I get a separate immigration attorney to coordinate with you?
- Given my change in status and that immigration court proceedings are pending, what immediate steps should we take so a settlement won’t harm my immigration case?
- Are there any admissions or factual statements we should avoid in pleadings or settlement language that could be used against me in immigration court?
- Can we delay signing any agreement until an immigration attorney has reviewed it (if you’re not handling immigration)?
- What temporary orders (support, custody, possession of home, restraining orders, insurance, bank access) should we request now to protect me before settlement?
- What are your recommended “non-negotiables” (red lines) for settlement given my immigration situation?
- How will a settlement affect any immigration sponsorship, pending I-130/I-485, conditional residency (I-751) or other immigration filings?
- Will any financial or custodial terms in the settlement create obligations or admissions that could affect my removal proceedings or benefit eligibility?

Family-law-specific questions
- How will community property / separate property be characterized and divided in my case? What evidence do you need to support my claims about assets/debts?
- How do you propose handling spousal support (amount, duration, tax treatment, modifiability, enforcement)?
- If there are children: custody/visitation plan, child support, health insurance, daycare and education costs, and which custodial arrangements would be safer from an immigration standpoint.
- How will retirement accounts, pensions, stock options, business interests, and tax liabilities be handled? Will a QDRO or other order be required?
- What are the standard protective contract clauses (indemnity, hold harmless, confidentiality, non-disparagement) and how should we tailor them?
- Are there automatic temporary restraining orders or other court rules in San Diego/California I should know about (freezing assets, sale of property, etc.)?

Immigration-specific questions to ask (if Amy is not an immigration specialist, ask whether she will coordinate)
- How could the proposed settlement language (admissions, custody agreements, financial arrangements) be used in my immigration case?
- If my spouse was or is my sponsor, what is the sponsor’s continuing obligations and how does divorce affect a pending visa/adjustment?
- If I have conditional permanent residency (2-year green card), will divorce affect removal of conditions? Do I need a waiver? What are the timelines?
- Could settlement terms expose me to greater risk of removal or negatively affect asylum/relief claims? Are there phrases to avoid in any agreement/pleadings?
- Will settlement (including payments) affect my eligibility for public benefits or forms of discretionary relief?
- What immigration documents, timelines, or hearings must be preserved/maintained while negotiations are ongoing?

Practical negotiation and strategy questions
- What is your recommended negotiation strategy (settlement vs. mediation vs. litigation) given my immigration exposure and timeline?
- What are likely trade-offs (money vs. immigration protection) and can we structure concessions to protect my immigration status first?
- If we reach a tentative settlement, can we make key immigration-sensitive clauses contingent upon immigration counsel review/approval?
- How long will negotiations likely take and how will they interface with the immigration court schedule? Are there critical deadlines we must meet?
- Who will pay costs of valuation experts, forensic accountants, or counsel coordination if required?

Documents to bring to your meeting (prepare copies and, if possible, a short chronological timeline)
- IDs and immigration documents (passports, visas, I-94, I-797, green card, receipts, any immigration filings and notices including the immigration court hearing notice)
- Marriage certificate, prenuptial/postnuptial agreements (if any)
- Recent tax returns (last 2–3 years), W-2s, 1099s, pay stubs, employer letters
- Bank and investment statements, retirement account statements, mortgage documents, deeds/title, vehicle titles
- Business records (if you or spouse own a business), profit & loss, corporate documents
- Credit card statements, loan agreements, lines of credit, debts
- Documents showing contributions to assets (large gifts, inheritances, databases of joint expenses)
- Any police reports, protective orders, or evidence of abuse (if relevant to immigration relief like VAWA)
- Correspondence or offers exchanged so far in settlement talks

Red flags to avoid in settlement language
- Broad releases that waive claims you may not understand, including immigration claims
- Admissions of fraud, misrepresentation, or false statements about immigration status or intent
- Language that says you will refrain from cooperating with immigration authorities or that binds you to make statements
- Agreed facts that conflict with your immigration testimony or filings
- Immediate large lump-sum payments that leave you without funds for legal defense or relocation
- Confidentiality terms that prevent you from seeking immigration counsel or reporting abuse

Follow-up actions to propose at your meeting
- Ask Amy to coordinate directly with an immigration attorney (get a referral if necessary) and intentionally draft any settlement contingency language for immigration review.
- Set immediate temporary orders to protect assets and access to funds.
- Get a prioritized list of documents Amy needs and a timeline for mandatory disclosures and the first hearing.
- Decide on your top 3 priorities (e.g., maintain immigration options, secure monthly support, retain home) so Amy negotiates with clear instructions.
- Ask Amy for an estimate of costs and timeline for both settlement and going to trial so you can weigh risks.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a concise email to Amy listing these questions and requesting a meeting before the first hearing.
- Create a printable checklist of the documents to bring.
- Help write suggested negotiation “non-negotiable” language you can propose (and then run by your lawyer).

Which of those would help most right now — draft the email to Amy, or create the document checklist?

