I can help — below is a practical, prioritized set of questions and a preparation checklist you can bring to your meeting with Amy Foster (and, where noted, to an immigration attorney). I’m not a lawyer, so treat these as organized prompts to make sure you cover topics that commonly matter in divorce + immigration cases.

Top priorities to clarify with your attorney
- How my change in immigration status affects the divorce process and settlement negotiations. Ask whether the divorce could jeopardize any pending immigration relief or petitions.
- Whether Amy will coordinate with an immigration attorney (or refer one) so settlement terms don’t create negative immigration consequences.
- What immediate protective steps I should take now (temporary orders, asset preservation, safety planning, confidentiality).
- What the likely timetable is (next hearing, discovery deadlines, temporary orders, mediation, settlement window, trial risk) and what I must file or respond to before the first hearing.

Suggested questions to ask Amy (grouped by topic)

Immigration-related
- How could my changed immigration status affect outcomes in the divorce (e.g., settlement terms, spousal support, affidavits of support, financial obligations)?
- Could anything I say or agree to in settlement be used against me in immigration court? If so, how can we avoid that?
- Will you coordinate or communicate with an immigration attorney on my behalf? If not, can you recommend one and work together before I sign anything?
- Are there specific settlement provisions we should avoid (or include) because of immigration consequences?
- If my immigration relief depends on my marriage (or spouse sponsorship), what legal options exist if we divorce (waivers, self-petitions, VAWA, other relief)? (Ask Amy to identify when immigration counsel is required.)

Settlement strategy and trade-offs
- What are my realistic settlement goals on property division, spousal support, and (if applicable) child custody/support?
- What are the biggest risks and benefits of settling vs. litigating?
- What concessions are commonly requested by the other side, and what concessions would you recommend I avoid?
- How do we value assets (home, retirement, business) and handle tax consequences and QDROs for retirement accounts?
- If the other party offers a settlement, how long do I have to review it and can we condition acceptance on immigration counsel review?

Financial and asset protection
- What temporary orders should we request now (temporary spousal support, child support, exclusive use of the home, asset freeze)?
- How do we protect joint bank accounts, credit cards, retirement accounts, and business interests?
- What emergency steps should I take (change passwords, separate finances, preserve documents)?
- Who will pay attorney fees, and can we request interim attorney fees if needed?

Custody/children (if applicable)
- What temporary custody and visitation arrangements should we request now?
- What information and documentation will help support my desired custody arrangement?
- How does child support calculation work here (San Diego/California specifics)?

Procedural and practical questions
- What will happen at the first hearing next month? Is it a status conference, temporary orders hearing, or something else? What should I bring or prepare?
- What documents or disclosures do I need to produce and by when? (California has mandatory disclosures — clarify timing and contents.)
- Do you recommend mediation or settlement conferences? When would those occur?
- What is your negotiation strategy and likely timeline to reach settlement?

Safety, confidentiality, and communications
- If there are safety concerns or domestic violence, how do we get protective orders and protect my immigration process?
- How should I communicate with the opposing party (directly, through counsel, or not at all)?
- Will our communications and settlement negotiations be protected/confidential? Any NDAs or confidentiality clauses to consider?

Evidence and documentation to gather (bring to your meeting)
- Immigration documents: passport, visa, I-94, green card, naturalization paperwork, receipts/filings (I-130, I-751, I-485, NTA or EOIR notices), A-number, notices from immigration court.
- Marriage certificate, any separation agreements, prior court orders.
- Financial records: bank statements, tax returns (3 years), pay stubs, employer letters, pension/retirement statements, mortgage statements, deeds, vehicle titles, credit card statements, debts, business records, investment accounts.
- Communications: emails, texts, social media messages relevant to separation, finances, or parenting.
- Evidence of abuse (if any): police reports, medical records, photos, witness names.
- Documents relating to children (birth certificates, school records, daycare costs).
- Any prenup/postnup agreements.

Practical checklist for the next meeting/hearing
- Ask Amy to summarize the specific purpose of the first hearing and what outcomes are possible that day.
- Bring the documents listed above (especially immigration docs and proof of income).
- Prepare a short prioritized list of what you must protect (e.g., immigration status, home, children, income).
- Ask Amy to identify any immediate filings she recommends (temporary orders, emergency motions) and deadlines.
- Confirm who will communicate with immigration counsel and whether we need immigration review before signing anything.

Questions about costs and communication
- What are your current hourly rates, retainer, and billing practices? How much do you expect the next 30–90 days to cost?
- How will you keep me updated (email, phone, client portal)? Who is the point of contact at the firm?

Language you can use in the meeting (short prompts)
- “Before I agree to any settlement terms, can you confirm whether any provision might impact my immigration case or require review by an immigration attorney?”
- “What immediate temporary orders do you recommend to protect my finances and housing?”
- “Could we list the non-negotiable items I must protect in a settlement, and where you recommend flexibility?”

Red flags to avoid without counsel/immigration review
- Signing anything that admits facts that could be used in immigration proceedings.
- Agreeing to give up immigration-related claims or benefits without immigration counsel review.
- Quick, pressured settlement offers that don’t address immigration consequences or financial protections.

Next step I can help with
- If you want, I can draft an email or short agenda you can send to Amy Foster to make your first meeting efficient — including the top immigration concerns and documents to bring. Would you like that?

