I can help you put together a focused, practical set of questions and a prep checklist so you get the most out of your meeting with Amy Foster and are ready for settlement talks and the upcoming hearing. I’m not a lawyer, so consider this general guidance — it’s a checklist you can bring to Amy and use to decide whether to get separate immigration counsel for the immigration-court aspects.

Top priorities to ask your attorney (urgent / must know now)
- How does my changed immigration status affect the divorce proceedings and any settlement terms?
- Could anything we agree to (language in the settlement or admissions) negatively affect my immigration case or eligibility for relief? If so, what language or admissions should I avoid?
- Do you recommend that I also consult an immigration attorney before agreeing to settlement terms or signing anything?
- Will a settlement or final judgment affect any pending immigration petitions (family sponsorship, I-751, cancellation of removal, VAWA, U, asylum, etc.)? If yes, how?
- Are there immediate steps to protect my immigration interests (filing waivers or motions, avoiding certain filings, asking the court to preserve rights)?

Questions about the first hearing and early process (what to expect / immediate next steps)
- What is the purpose of the first hearing in our case and what are likely outcomes (scheduling, temporary orders, discovery deadlines, settlement conference)?
- What filings, disclosures, or forms must we have prepared before the hearing (California preliminary disclosures, Income & Expense Declaration, Schedule of Assets and Debts)?
- Are temporary orders appropriate here (spousal support, exclusive use of home, temporary custody, restraining orders)? If so, when should we request them?
- What deadlines should I be aware of (discovery, financial disclosures, mediation, hearing dates)?

Settlement-specific questions to protect your interests
- What are the key items to negotiate in settlement (division of property and debts, spousal support, health insurance, retirement accounts/QDROs, tax treatment, attorney fees)?
- How would California community-property law likely apply to our assets and debts?
- What specific language should be included to protect me (e.g., that transfers are subject to court approval, escrow instructions, non-waiver of immigration claims)?
- Should settlement terms be entered as a court order (so they are enforceable) rather than only a private agreement?
- How will retirement accounts, pension, or vested benefits be handled — will we need a QDRO or pension-specific order?
- How will taxes be handled (who pays tax on asset sales, division of tax refunds, alimony tax treatment)? Should I consult a tax professional?

Immigration-focused settlement issues and wording
- Will the settlement require me to waive any immigration claims or rights? If so, is that waiver advisable?
- Should we include a clause preserving my ability to disclose immigration status to the immigration court or not waive any immigration relief?
- If my ex-spouse was or is a sponsor on an immigrant petition, will divorce terminate the petition or sponsorship obligations? What steps protect any pending petitions?
- If there are allegations of domestic violence or abuse, how will that be handled with respect to immigration relief (VAWA, protective filings)?
- Are there any admissions (financial, residency, intent) that could be used against me in immigration proceedings — what language should we avoid?

Discovery, evidence, and documentation to prepare and bring to the lawyer/hearing
- IDs and immigration papers (passports, green card, I-94, notice to appear, A-number, any immigration filings and receipts)
- Marriage certificate and any divorce/ separation paperwork
- Recent pay stubs, employer letter, and last 2–3 years’ tax returns (federal & state)
- Bank and investment account statements (last 12–24 months)
- Mortgage statements, deeds, property valuations, lease agreements
- Retirement/401(k)/pension statements
- Credit card and loan statements, outstanding debt records
- Proof of monthly expenses, rent/mortgage, utilities, insurance
- Communications with ex-spouse that are relevant (text/email copies), and any evidence of domestic violence or threats (police reports, medical records)
- Any immigration-court documents, filings, hearing notices, or orders

Settlement negotiation strategy and practical protections
- Prioritize what you must keep vs. what you can concede (home, savings, support, immigration protection).
- Consider getting an immigration lawyer involved early to advise on immigration consequences of settlement terms.
- Use precise, limited release language in the settlement; avoid broad waivers that could impair future immigration claims.
- Insist on court entry of settlement terms if you want enforceability (e.g., support payments entered as judgments).
- Consider escrow or phased transfers for large assets instead of immediate transfers.
- Include attorney-fee and enforcement provisions (what happens if the other party stops paying).
- Keep communication documented and preferably go through counsel for settlement negotiations.

Questions about costs, timeline, and representation
- What are your estimated costs and timeline if we attempt settlement vs. if the case goes to trial?
- What is your settlement strategy and bottom-line position you’d recommend?
- Are you comfortable handling the immigration intersection, or do you recommend co-counsel with an immigration specialist?
- How will you communicate settlement offers to me and do you have a protocol for when to accept/ counter?

Court/jurisdiction/enforcement details to confirm
- Does the settlement need to specify California law or San Diego County court for enforcement?
- How are out-of-state or cross-border enforcement issues handled (if relevant to immigration or asset location)?
- Does entry of judgment affect immigration filings (for example, evidence of financial support for immigration applications)?

Checklist to take to your meeting with Amy Foster
- Copy of all immigration-court documents and notices
- Copy of marriage certificate and IDs
- Last 2–3 years’ tax returns and current pay stubs
- Last 12 months’ bank/investment statements
- Mortgage/loan/credit-card statements
- Retirement/pension statements
- Any communications with ex-spouse relevant to the case
- List of questions prioritized (urgent immigration consequences first)
- Note about what you must protect (e.g., ability to stay, specific assets, custody, safety)

Sample short questions you can read at the meeting
- “Can you explain how my recent change in immigration status affects our legal options and the settlement?”
- “Before I sign anything, can you confirm whether any settlement language could be used against me in immigration court?”
- “Should we get an immigration attorney involved now? If so, can you recommend someone?”
- “What temporary orders should I seek before the first hearing?”
- “Which financial disclosures must be filed now, and by when?”

Would you like me to:
- Draft an email to Amy Foster listing these questions and asking for a meeting before the hearing?
- Turn this into a printable one-page checklist you can bring to the meeting?
- Or help you draft specific settlement language to discuss with Amy (not legal advice, but suggested phrasing to ask about)?

Tell me which of those you want and I’ll prepare it.

