I can help you get organized. Below are focused questions and a checklist you can use when you meet with Amy Foster (and with any immigration counsel you involve). I’ve grouped items so you can prioritize what to ask at your next meeting and what to prepare before the hearing next month.

High-level preparation (what to decide before the meeting)
- Your goals and “non‑negotiables”: e.g., custody arrangements, keeping the house, protecting immigration options, minimum spousal/child support, safety concerns.
- Prioritize: must-have, desirable, and deal-breaker items for settlement.
- Note any immediate risks to your immigration status (e.g., conditional status, pending petitions, work auth expiring, active removal/immigration court matters).

Questions to ask Amy Foster (organized by topic)

Case basics and process
- What are the next procedural steps in both the family court and how this interacts with my immigration case? What will happen at the first hearing?
- What temporary orders should we seek (or oppose) now — e.g., temporary child custody, spousal support, restraining orders, exclusive use of residence?
- What is the expected timeline for settlement discussions, temporary orders, disclosures, and trial?
- Should we ask for a continuance of the first hearing to allow more time for discovery/settlement?

Settlement strategy
- From your experience in San Diego County, what settlement approach is most effective here (mediation, settlement conference, direct negotiation)?
- What are realistic settlement ranges for spousal support, property division, and child support in my situation?
- What concessions are reasonable to make — and which should I avoid because they could harm my immigration position?
- Do you recommend confidentiality/non-disparagement clauses? How might they affect future immigration testimony or applications?

