I can help you get organized and prepare a focused list of questions and action items to discuss with Amy Foster so you go into settlement talks and the upcoming hearing ready. Below are suggested questions, documents to gather, immediate steps, negotiation points, and red flags — all organized so you can use them in your meeting or email to Amy.

Important: this is general guidance to help you prepare. For legal conclusions about your specific case and immigration consequences, rely on Amy (and an immigration attorney if she isn’t handling immigration-specific issues).

1) Priority questions to ask your attorney (Amy Foster)
- Case status & timeline
  - What is the exact procedural posture in both family/divorce and immigration court? What will happen at next month’s hearing?
  - What deadlines should I calendar now (discovery, disclosures, settlement conference, mediation, trial readiness)?
- Settlement strategy & goals
  - Based on our goals, what’s your recommended settlement strategy (settle now, seek temporary orders first, mediate)?
  - What are my must-haves vs. acceptable trade-offs?
  - What is a realistic range for spousal support, property division, and other key monetary issues in San Diego County given our circumstances?
- Immigration-specific implications
  - How will a divorce or any settlement terms affect my immigration status or pending immigration case(s)?
  - Should we involve or consult an immigration attorney before agreeing to anything that could affect my status?
  - If my status changed recently, what specific risks should I be aware of (loss of sponsorship, eligibility for benefits, impact on pending applications, ability to travel)?
- Temporary orders & protection now
  - Should we seek temporary orders now for support, custody, exclusive use of the home, or prohibitions on asset transfers?
  - Can we obtain immediate protections (restraining order, asset freeze) if needed?
- Financial matters & disclosure
  - What financial documentation will the other side be required to produce, and how do we get relevant documentation quickly?
  - How should I handle joint accounts, joint debts, retirement accounts, and property until the case is resolved?
- Custody/children (if applicable)
  - What temporary custody/visitation orders do you recommend until settlement or trial?
  - How will custody decisions affect my immigration status (if at all)?
- Settlement terms & drafting
  - What specific clauses should be included to protect my immigration interests (cooperation clauses, language about sponsorship, etc.)?
  - How should we structure confidentiality, release, non-disparagement, and enforcement provisions?
- Fees, costs, and risk
  - What are the likely attorney fees and costs if we proceed to mediation/trial vs. settling now?
  - Do we include an attorneys’ fees clause in the settlement to deter noncompliance?
- Communication & coordination
  - How will you coordinate with any immigration counsel and me? Who will take the lead on immigration vs family law issues?
  - How should I communicate with the opposing party (or their counsel) during negotiations?
- Documents and evidence
  - What documents or evidence would most strengthen my position (financial, communications, proof of contributions, evidence of domestic or financial abuse, etc.)?
- Settlement procedure & acceptance
  - If a settlement offer is received, how quickly can/will you review it? What process do you use to evaluate offers?
  - Are there common traps to avoid when signing settlement documents that could affect immigration/status or future rights?

2) Documents to gather and bring to your meeting
- Identity & relationship: marriage certificate, divorce filings (if any), birth certificates of children
- Immigration documents: passports, visas, green card, I-130/I-485 forms and receipts, notices from immigration court (NTA), any communications with USCIS/EOIR
- Financial statements (last 2–3 years if possible): tax returns (federal and state), W-2s/1099s, pay stubs, bank statements, brokerage statements, retirement account statements (401k, IRA), mortgage/loan statements, recent credit card statements
- Property documents: deed/title, mortgage, vehicle titles, lease agreements
- Debts: loan documents, promissory notes, credit agreements
- Business documents (if applicable): business tax returns, profit & loss, ownership agreements
- Support records: records of spousal/child support paid or received, health insurance coverage, child care expenses
- Communications and evidence: relevant emails, text messages, social media posts, photos, recordings (note legal restrictions), any evidence of threats/abuse or coercion
- Court & legal documents: any temporary orders, restraining orders, prior court filings, correspondence with opposing counsel

3) Immediate steps to take now (before the hearing)
- Preserve evidence: save and back up emails, texts, photos, and documents. Do not delete relevant communications.
- Avoid making admissions: do not sign agreements or make statements (especially about immigration status) without checking with Amy or immigration counsel.
- Freeze risky transactions: avoid large transfers or withdrawals from joint accounts and avoid giving away property without legal advice.
- Restrict social media: tighten privacy settings and avoid posting about the case, finances, or travel.
- Notify Amy of any change in immigration notices immediately.
- Consider whether you should request a continuance if you need time to coordinate with immigration counsel or gather documents (ask Amy).

4) Negotiation points and settlement provisions to request or consider
- Clear timelines and amounts for spousal support: duration, method of payment, tax treatment
- Property division specifics: who gets which assets, how to value and divide retirement or business interests, who pays debts
- Health insurance continuation and who pays for premiums
- Tax considerations: who bears liabilities, filing status for relevant years, responsibility for past-due taxes
- Custody and parenting plan specifics (if children): visitation schedule, decision-making authority, child support, insurance, education decisions
- Enforcement & modification terms: how to enforce the agreement (court enforcement, attorneys’ fees), and when modification is allowed
- Confidentiality and non-disparagement clauses (narrowly tailored)
- Immigration-specific language: cooperation provisions for immigration forms, explicit statements that the settlement isn’t intended to harm pending immigration relief (phrase with your attorney), and whether to include any assistance for immigration filings
- Release language: careful with broad releases that could inadvertently waive rights related to immigration or future claims

5) Questions to clarify about hearings and courtroom behavior
- What will happen at the first hearing? What should I expect to say or do?
- Will there be an opportunity to set temporary orders? Can we present an agreed temporary order?
- Should I testify at the first hearing or wait?
- How should I dress and behave in court? Who will speak for me?
- Are there any risks in attending immigration court hearings while litigation is pending in family court?

6) Red flags to watch for during settlement talks
- Pressure to sign quickly or without full financial disclosure
- Language that broadly waives future claims without clear limits
- Transfer of immigration documents or control over evidence
- Offers that seem to benefit the other party disproportionately without explanation
- Requests that you relinquish spousal immigration-related claims without immigration counsel input

7) Suggested agenda for your meeting with Amy (to keep it focused)
- Brief factual recap and any changes since last contact (immigration notices, income changes, threats)
- Review of top priorities and must-have outcomes
- Review/collect documents needed immediately
- Discuss immigration risks and whether to involve immigration counsel
- Map short-term steps before the hearing (temporary orders, discovery, evidence preservation)
- Outline next actions and who does what, with deadlines

8) Sample wording for emails or questions during your meeting
- “Can you explain how a divorce settlement could affect my current immigration application, and should we consult an immigration attorney before agreeing to any settlement terms?”
- “What temporary orders do you recommend seeking before the next hearing to protect my housing, finances, and custody position?”
- “Please list the top 10 documents you need from me right away so I can gather them before our next meeting.”

9) Offer I can do for you now
- I can draft a concise email you can send to Amy summarizing your concerns and requesting a prep meeting (include list of documents to bring and key questions).
- I can turn the prioritized questions into a one-page checklist you can take to your appointment or court.
- I can draft suggested language to propose in settlement negotiations that you can review with Amy (but you must check and approve with her).

Would you like me to:
- Draft the email to Amy Foster requesting a prep meeting and listing the documents/questions? Or
- Create a one-page checklist you can print and take to your meeting/hearing?

Tell me which and I’ll draft it right away.

