I can help you organize what to ask and prepare so you’re ready for settlement talks and the upcoming hearing. Below is a structured checklist: priority questions to ask Amy, documents to bring, things to confirm before settlement, suggested phrasing for questions, and immediate next steps. I’m giving general guidance — your attorney should confirm how it applies to your exact facts and immigration status.

Priority questions to ask your attorney (Amy Foster)
- Case status & next steps
  - What is the goal for the next hearing? What should I expect (temporary orders, status conference, settlement conference, scheduling)?
  - What exactly will you ask the court for at the first hearing?
  - What deadlines do I need to know (discovery, financial disclosures, mediation, settlement conference)?

- Immigration-specific implications
  - How does my changed immigration status affect the divorce process and possible settlement terms?
  - Could any settlement language or a divorce itself affect my immigration relief or pending immigration applications/immigration court case?
  - Are there particular clauses I should avoid in a settlement so I don’t jeopardize immigration options (e.g., admissions of fact, waivers)?
  - If you’re not handling immigration law, can you coordinate with or refer me to an immigration attorney who can advise on the settlement terms?

- Financial/property division (California: community property)
  - How will assets and debts likely be divided? What’s community property vs separate property in our situation?
  - What documentation do you need to value assets (bank, retirement, property, business)?
  - How will retirement accounts, pensions, stock options, and tax consequences be handled? Will a QDRO be needed?
  - How are hidden or transferred assets uncovered and handled?

- Support (temporary and permanent)
  - Should I ask for temporary spousal support or exclusive use of the house before final settlement?
  - How is temporary spousal support calculated? How long does it typically last?
  - What factors affect permanent spousal support here?

- Children (if applicable)
  - What’s the plan for custody/visitation and child support in the short and long term?
  - Should we seek temporary custody orders now?
  - What information will the court want about child welfare, schooling, and expenses?

- Settlement strategy and terms
  - What is your recommended settlement strategy (aggressive, compromise, mediation)?
  - What is my bottom line and which items can I be flexible on?
  - Should we use a mediator? What are the costs/time?
  - What typical clauses protect me (enforcement, spousal support modifications, tax allocations, confidentiality, release language)?
  - Can we include provisions to protect my immigration situation (e.g., statements that do not constitute immigration admissions)?

- Confidentiality, releases, and consequences
  - What release language would be safe/unsafe for my immigration or criminal exposure?
  - If I accept a lump-sum payment or waive support, what are the long-term consequences?

- Fees, timeline, and communication
  - What are your estimated fees and costs through the next hearing and through settlement/trial?
  - Who will represent me in court if you are unavailable?
  - How will you communicate updates and what do you need from me?

Documents and evidence to bring to your meeting and to prepare
- Marriage certificate, any prenuptial/postnuptial agreements
- Birth certificates of children (if any)
- All immigration documents: I-94, green card, any petitions (I-130, VAWA, asylum, cancellations), notices from immigration court, work authorization, any correspondence with ICE/immigration counsel
- Court documents served or filed so far (petition, summons, any immigration filings)
- Financial records (last 3 years tax returns, recent pay stubs, W-2s, bank statements last 12 months, credit card statements, mortgage statements, deeds)
- Retirement account statements, brokerage accounts, stock option documents, business records
- Bills and monthly expenses (mortgage/rent, utilities, childcare, medical)
- Evidence of contributions to assets (large gifts, inheritances)
- Records of communications (texts, emails) relevant to settlement or property division
- Evidence of abuse/domestic violence, if any (police reports, medical records, restraining orders, witness names)
- Any transfer/sale records of property in the past few years

Practical settlement items to confirm or negotiate
- Explicit protection for immigration-related issues (language that nothing in the settlement is an admission impacting immigration eligibility)
- Who pays debts and how they’re divided
- Allocation of tax liabilities (who takes which deductions/credits for past/future tax years)
- Timing and method of payments (lump sum vs installments), enforcement mechanisms, late-payment penalties, security (liens)
- Retirement division mechanism (QDRO) and who handles the preparation
- Health insurance continuation and who pays children’s expenses
- Attorney fees and costs allocation
- Confidentiality/non-disparagement clauses and whether they could conflict with immigration disclosures or court obligations

Court and hearing preparation (for next month)
- Ask Amy what filings or financial disclosures must be in before the first hearing and confirm deadlines
- Ask whether you should seek temporary orders (support, exclusive occupancy, restraining order)
- Make sure you have a timeline of required documents and any declarations you must prepare
- Confirm whether to be present and whether you or Amy should prepare a short statement for the judge

Suggested phrasing you can use with Amy
- “Given my change in immigration status and that I’m in removal proceedings, can we review settlement language to ensure it won’t jeopardize any immigration relief?”
- “What temporary orders do you recommend we request at the first hearing to protect my housing and income while this proceeds?”
- “What documents should I provide right now so we can prepare complete financial disclosures by the deadline?”
- “If I accept a settlement, how will that affect any pending immigration applications or my chances in immigration court?”
- “Do you recommend mediation? If so, when should we schedule it and what is our negotiation bottom line?”

Red flags to avoid in settlement talks
- Signing releases that broadly waive future claims without clear definitions or protections for immigration consequences
- Agreeing to odd structured payments without enforcement mechanisms or security (e.g., no lien, no collateral)
- Accepting oral agreements — insist on written, signed settlement documents
- Failing to involve an immigration attorney if immigration status or relief is at stake

Immediate next steps I recommend you do now
- Send Amy a concise email/attachment listing these questions and ask for a meeting agenda so the first hearing is productive.
- Gather the documents listed above and upload them to Amy’s secure portal or bring to the meeting.
- Confirm whether Amy will coordinate with an immigration lawyer; if not, ask for a referral immediately.
- Identify any immediate safety concerns (domestic violence) that would require emergency orders.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a concise email or meeting agenda to send to Amy listing the top questions and documents? (I can draft it in plain language or more formal.)
- Create a one-page checklist you can bring to the first hearing?
- Or do you want to answer a couple quick questions so I can tailor the list (e.g., do you have children, what changed about your immigration status, is Amy handling immigration issues too)?

Tell me which of those you want and I’ll prepare it.

