I can help. Below is a focused, practical set of things to ask Amy Foster and to prepare before settlement talks and your upcoming hearing. I’ve grouped items so you can go into the meeting organized and ready to prioritize.

High‑priority facts to clarify with your attorney (ask these first)
- How does my changed immigration status affect the legal options and timing in both the divorce and the immigration court case? (If Amy isn’t your immigration lawyer, ask whether she will coordinate with immigration counsel.)
- Will any settlement terms (admissions, custody arrangements, waivers, releases) affect my eligibility for immigration relief or applications? If so, how?
- What immediate protections should we file for now (temporary orders, asset preservation, injunctions, restraining orders) before settlement negotiations proceed?
- Do you recommend we delay settlement talks until we resolve aspects of the immigration case (or vice versa)? What are the risks and benefits of settling now?

Key settlement questions to ask (use or adapt these in your meeting)
Immigration-specific
- Will the settlement language or any admissions be shared with immigration court/USCIS? Could a signed agreement harm my immigration case?
- Does a divorce or particular settlement term affect any pending immigration relief I may be eligible for (adjustment of status, VAWA, cancellation, etc.)?
- Should we get a separate immigration lawyer review any proposed settlement before I sign?
- Are there safe wording options we can use that protect my immigration position (e.g., non-admission clauses, confidentiality, narrow releases)?

Financial & property division (California law)
- What property is community property vs. separate property given our situation and dates? How will assets be valued (date of separation)?
- What documents do you need (tax returns, bank statements, real estate deeds, retirement accounts, business valuations, stock options, mortgages)?
- How should retirement/pension accounts, stock options, or business interests be handled? Will we need a QDRO or equivalent?
- How is debt allocated? Are there steps to prevent one party from taking or hiding assets before settlement?
- How will settlements be taxed? Are there tax consequences I should plan for (alimony vs property transfer, sale of real estate)?

Support (spousal & child)
- What is a likely spousal support outcome (amount, duration)? What factors matter most here in San Diego/California?
- If children are involved: how will custody, visitation, and child support be handled? What evidence will be decisive?
- If support is part of the settlement, what enforcement mechanisms should we include?

Custody, safety, and family issues
- If there are children, how will custody/visitation terms interact with immigration exposure or potential deportation risks?
- Should we include safety/communication/transportation specifics in the settlement (supervised pickup, restrictions, emergency provisions)?
- Should we request temporary custody/visitation orders before settlement?

Procedural & timing
- What are the deadlines for disclosures and discovery in our case? What must be produced before settlement talks?
- What is the timeline to the next hearing and what do you recommend we accomplish before then?
- If negotiations break down, what will happen at the next hearing? Do you recommend requesting a continuance?
- What settlement negotiation strategy do you recommend (positioning, bottom line, concessions we can/cannot make)?

Drafting/terms to include in any settlement/agreements
- Confidentiality vs public court order: should settlement be a confidential agreement or entered as a court order? (Tradeoffs: enforceability vs privacy.)
- Non‑dissipation clause (prohibit spending/transferring assets while negotiations pending).
- Clear enforcement language (attorney’s fees for enforcement, choice of law and jurisdiction—San Diego).
- Specifics for division of bank accounts, retirement, business, real estate, vehicles.
- Tax allocation clause (who gets tax deductions/credits for children, property sale proceeds).
- Survivability clauses (which obligations survive divorce).
- Narrow release language that does not waive immigration claims or future immigration relief.
- If spousal support: clear payment method, duration, modification terms, and enforcement remedies.

Evidence & documents to bring to your meeting (gather these now)
- Government immigration paperwork (notices from EOIR/USCIS, I-797s, prior applications).
- Full tax returns (last 3 years), most recent pay stubs, W-2s, 1099s.
- Bank and credit card statements (last 12–24 months).
- Mortgage statements, deeds/title, car titles, retirement account statements, stock/ESOP documents.
- Business records (profit & loss, balance sheets) if either party owns a business.
- Any prenup/postnup or prior agreements.
- Communications relevant to disputes (texts, emails).
- Court filings and notices in the immigration case and in family court.

Negotiation strategy and red flags
- Insist on review by immigration counsel before signing anything that could affect status.
- Avoid broad releases or admissions of fault that could be used in immigration proceedings.
- Protect cash flow and essentials — secure temporary support if you need it.
- Be cautious with lump-sum buyouts if you lack cash or the other party could hide assets.
- Don’t sign anything under time pressure without full explanation and counsel.

Practical next steps to ask Amy to take
- File any needed temporary orders (support, custody, asset freeze) immediately if applicable.
- Send written discovery requests and demand financial disclosures.
- Propose a written settlement framework outlining major terms she can negotiate.
- Coordinate with/ refer an immigration attorney and set up a joint strategy call.
- Prepare an evidence packet for the next hearing and a plan for settlement vs hearing.

Other questions about representation and logistics
- What is your fee structure for negotiations vs court hearings? What additional costs should I expect?
- How will we communicate (email/phone/client portal)? What’s your expected response time?
- Who will attend the hearing with me, and what should I expect at the hearing next month?

Would you like me to:
- Turn this into a one‑page checklist you can print and bring to your appointment?
- Draft a short email to Amy listing these priorities and asking for a meeting or confirmation of next steps?
- Or tailor the questions further (for example, if there are children, a business, or a sponsor affidavit)?

If you want me to draft an email or printable checklist, tell me which of the above you want included and any other facts (e.g., children? joint home? were you sponsored by your ex?) and I’ll prepare it.

