I can help — below is a focused, practical checklist you can use to prepare for settlement talks and your next meeting/hearing with Amy Foster. I avoid giving specific legal conclusions (those come from your lawyer), but I list the important topics, questions to ask, documents to bring, and immediate steps to consider so you can protect your interests.

Top-priority questions to ask Amy at your next meeting
- How will settlement negotiations interact with the immigration court proceedings? What immediate immigration risks do I face from a divorce or settlement now?  
- Based on my current immigration status, what specific immigration consequences could result from a divorce (e.g., termination of sponsorship, impact on pending applications, conditional residence issues)?  
- Should we coordinate with an immigration specialist? Do you coordinate with an immigration attorney or can you refer one?  
- What temporary orders should we seek before settlement (temporary spousal support, child support, exclusive use of the home, restraining order, freeze on bank accounts or transfers)?  
- What should I ask for in a settlement to protect my financial and immigration interests (amount/length of spousal support, health insurance continuation, allocation of debts, tax treatment, QDRO for retirement accounts)?  
- How do we ensure the settlement terms are enforceable (language, escrow, enforcement clauses, penalties for noncompliance)?  
- What information will the opposing party likely demand in discovery? What should we proactively prepare or withhold?  
- Do you recommend mediation or direct settlement talks? What is our BATNA (best alternative to negotiated agreement) and what’s an acceptable bottom line?  
- If there’s any domestic violence, coercion, or fear of retaliation, what immediate protective steps and disclosures are needed?  
- Timeline: what will happen at the first hearing next month, what do we need to have ready by then, and what should be our short-term milestones?  

Specific immigration-focused questions (ask if they apply to you)
- Given my current status (please tell Amy exactly what it is), does a divorce end any immigration benefit I currently have or one that is pending?  
- If I am/was a conditional permanent resident, will I need a waiver of the joint filing (I-751) and how does a divorce affect that process?  
- If my spouse previously sponsored me (I-130, adjustment, etc.), what consequences should I expect, and are there alternative pathways I should explore (self-petitions, humanitarian waivers, etc.)?  
- Can settlement language or timing be used to reduce immigration risk (e.g., delay finalization until immigration is resolved)? Is that advisable?  
- Will reaching a settlement trigger disclosure to immigration authorities or affect my immigration court case?  

Documents to bring to the meeting / to prepare for settlement talks
- All immigration documents and notices (green card, work/advance parole, I-797s, I-130 receipt/approval, I-485 receipts, immigration court notices, any communications with ICE/EOIR).  
- Marriage certificate, any separation agreement, prenup/postnup.  
- Recent pay stubs (last 3 months), last 3 years’ tax returns (personal & business), W-2s, 1099s.  
- Bank statements (last 12 months), credit card statements, mortgage statements, lease agreements.  
- Retirement & investment account statements, pension info, stock/options statements.  
- Vehicle titles, deed(s) to property, appraisal if available.  
- Loan documents and all debt statements.  
- Any evidence of payments between you and spouse (transfers, support, household expenses).  
- Communications related to immigration or divorce (texts, emails) and any evidence of coercion, threats, or abuse if relevant.  
- Child-related documents if children are involved (birth certificates, school records, evidence of primary care).  

Settlement terms and protective language to consider asking about
- Temporary relief while negotiations continue: temporary support, exclusive use of residence, prohibitions on liquidating assets.  
- Clear allocation of assets and debts, including date of valuation and who pays what tax liabilities.  
- Spousal support: amount, duration, tax treatment, conditions for modification/termination.  
- Treatment of retirement accounts (QDROs), stock options, and business interests — who keeps what and how transfers are executed.  
- Health insurance continuation and who covers premiums until resolution.  
- Confidentiality clauses, releases, and whether settlement will require disclosures to immigration authorities.  
- Enforcement mechanisms (liquidated damages, escrow, attorney’s fees, jurisdiction clause favoring San Diego County).  
- Non-financial terms you may need (parenting plan, custody/visitation, move-away language).  
- A clause preserving your right to pursue immigration remedies or clarifying that specific settlement items won’t prejudice immigration claims, if possible. (Ask Amy if this is feasible/legal.)

Practical negotiation strategy questions
- What is your recommended opening position and what concessions are acceptable?  
- Should we make an offer that conditions divorce finalization on a separate immigration-safe step?  
- How do we handle confidentiality vs. the need to preserve evidence for immigration court?  
- Who will do the heavy lifting on discovery (financial forensic accountant, private investigator) and when should we engage them?  

Immediate steps you can take now
- Gather the documents above and provide copies to Amy well before the hearing.  
- Ask Amy for a short meeting or call to go over immigration specifics so she can advise whether immigration counsel is required.  
- Do not sign any settlement or immigration-related documents from the ex without discussing them with Amy.  
- Freeze joint accounts or change passwords only after consulting Amy (to avoid claims of concealment).  
- If you feel threatened, ask Amy about emergency protective orders and whether to alert immigration counsel.  

Questions I need from you to tailor this further
- What is your current immigration status (citizen, lawful permanent resident — conditional or not, nonimmigrant, pending application, detained, etc.)?  
- Are there children involved? Custody concerns?  
- Any history or allegations of domestic violence, abuse, or threats?  
- Is there a prenuptial or postnuptial agreement?  
- Do you want me to draft an email or meeting agenda to Amy with these questions and attach the documents checklist?

