I can help — below is a focused, practical set of questions and a prep checklist you can use with Amy Foster (or any attorney) so you’re ready for settlement talks and the upcoming immigration hearing. I’ve grouped items so you can pick what’s most relevant and bring the right documents.

High-level goals to cover with Amy
- Confirm the legal strategy for both the divorce settlement and the immigration case and how they affect each other.
- Identify immediate protective steps (financial, evidentiary, and court orders) so the opposing party can’t sabotage your settlement position or immigration options.
- Make sure any settlement terms won’t unintentionally harm your immigration status or future relief options.

Questions to ask your attorney (use these in your meeting or email)
A. Case status & strategy
- What is our overall strategy for the divorce and how does it coordinate with the immigration case at immigration court?
- What are the realistic outcomes we should expect in settlement talks vs going to trial/hearing?
- What is the timeline (next steps, deadlines, the next hearing date) and what must we do before each milestone?

B. Immigration-specific
- How does my change in immigration status affect the divorce and the immigration proceedings?
- Will any settlement terms (financial support, release clauses, waiver of claims) affect my eligibility for relief (adjustment of status, cancellation of removal, VAWA, U visa, etc.)?
- If my ex-spouse was or is a sponsor, how will divorce affect any pending family-based petitions (I-130) or conditional residence (e.g., I-751)?
- Are there immigration benefits or protections we should seek to preserve in the settlement (e.g., a statement of support, cooperation for petitions, or an agreed timeline)?
- Should I avoid admitting anything or signing certain statements that could be used against me in immigration court?
- Do we need to involve or coordinate with an immigration specialist if you aren’t handling immigration directly?

C. Financial & property division
- How will California community-property rules apply to our assets and debts?
- What is the proposed split of assets, retirement accounts, bank accounts, and debts? Are those splits enforceable?
- Should we seek temporary orders to prevent asset dissipation (freeze accounts, injunction, TRO)?
- Who will pay attorney fees and court costs? Can I request interim spousal support?
- Tax consequences: how will the settlement affect my taxes (filing status, liabilities, and tax on property transfers)?

D. Spousal support & ongoing obligations
- What spousal support (temporary and permanent) is reasonable and how is it calculated here?
- How long would support last and what triggers termination?
- How will any support payments be documented to satisfy immigration or other legal requirements?

E. Children/custody/support (if applicable)
- What custody and child support proposals should we consider?
- How will custody agreements affect my immigration case or ability to travel?
- Are there safety/relocation clauses we should include?

F. Confidentiality, releases, and non-compete/non-disparagement
- Should the settlement include confidentiality or non-disparagement clauses? Are these enforceable here?
- Will a release/waiver in the settlement prevent me from pursuing future immigration relief or claims?
- Can we add a carve-out preserving my right to pursue immigration relief or criminal/ex parte matters?

G. Evidence, discovery & documentation
- What documents and evidence should I collect and preserve now (financial records, communications, proof of abuse if relevant)?
- How will we exchange discovery? Are there pending discovery requests?
- How do we protect sensitive immigration documents when producing materials?

H. Risks & what to avoid
- What specific settlement language or concessions would you advise I never sign (examples)?
- What are the likely tactics the opposing party will use in settlement talks and in immigration court?

I. Enforcement & modifications
- How will the settlement be enforced (court order, wage garnishment, contempt)?
- If my immigration status changes again, can we modify the settlement later?

J. Practicalities & communication
- How should I communicate with you and how quickly can I expect responses?
- Should any communication with my ex be done only through counsel?
- Will you coordinate with any immigration counsel or other specialists?

Documents and materials to bring to your meeting
- Photo ID, passports, immigration documents (I-94, I-797, pending petitions/forms, notices from immigration court), any notices from EOIR/ICE
- Marriage certificate and any prior separation agreements or prenup/postnup
- Any divorce pleadings already filed (petition, summons, temporary orders)
- Bank statements (last 2–3 years), recent pay stubs, employer info, tax returns (last 2–3 years)
- Retirement account statements, 401(k), IRAs, pension documents
- Mortgage statements, deeds or property titles, vehicle titles and loan statements
- Credit card statements and other debt documents
- Lease agreements, insurance policies (health, life), beneficiary designations
- Records of transfers of large sums, recent account closures, or asset sales
- Communications with your ex (texts, emails, messages) that are relevant (harassment, threats, admissions)
- Any evidence related to abuse, coercion, or criminal charges (police reports, medical records)
- Notices or filings from immigration court (master calendar hearing notice, charging document)

Immediate actions to consider now
- Don’t sign any settlement or immigration-related document without reviewing it with Amy or immigration counsel.
- Freeze or monitor accounts if you suspect the opposing party might try to move/dissipate assets; ask attorney about temporary restraining or protective orders.
- Preserve all communications and back them up (screenshots, printouts).
- Secure originals of important documents (immigration, IDs, deeds).
- Check your current work authorization and travel restrictions; don’t intentionally travel without legal advice.
- If you fear retaliation or safety issues, discuss protective orders or additional safety planning.

How I can help next
- I can turn this checklist into a concise email or agenda to send to Amy Foster before your meeting.
- I can draft a short script or bullet points you can read in the hearing or during settlement talks.
- If you’d like, tell me whether you have children, whether Amy is also handling your immigration case, and any specific immigration status details you’re comfortable sharing (e.g., pending I-130, detained, adjustment of status in progress). With that I’ll tailor the questions and document list further.

Would you like me to draft an email to Amy summarizing these questions and requesting a meeting or clarification on specific immigration implications?

