I can help. Below is a practical, organized list of questions and a document checklist to bring to your attorney (Amy Foster). I’ve grouped items by topic so you can prioritize what matters most before your first hearing next month. If you want, I can also draft an email or meeting agenda you can send to Amy with these questions.

Top priorities (ask these first)
- How does my changed immigration status affect the divorce and the immigration case? What immediate immigration risks should I know about (e.g., detention, removal, loss of work authorization, travel restrictions)?
- What temporary orders should we ask the court for now (temporary spousal support, child custody/visitation, restraining order, exclusive use of the home)?
- How could any settlement terms affect my immigration case? Are there specific clauses I should avoid or require to protect my immigration interests?

Detailed questions to ask your attorney

Case overview & timeline
- What are the next procedural steps in both the family/divorce case and the immigration case? How do they interact?
- What should I expect at the first hearing next month (purpose, duration, who will speak, typical outcomes)?
- What deadlines do I need to know and what paperwork must be filed before the hearing?

Immigration-specific questions
- Given my change in status, how will divorce affect any pending applications/petitions (adjustment of status, naturalization, VAWA, removal defense, etc.)?
- If I am or was sponsored, how does any sponsorship (e.g., I-864 affidavit of support) affect settlement and post-divorce obligations?
- If my residency was conditional on marriage, what are the options (waivers, I-751, evidence) if the marriage ends?
- Am I eligible for any immigration relief tied to domestic violence or abuse (VAWA) or other humanitarian relief (U visa, cancellation of removal)? If so, how does settlement or testimony interact with those applications?
- Could settling certain financial terms or signing a confidentiality clause hurt my immigration case or my credibility in immigration court?
- Should we coordinate with the immigration attorney (if different)? Would you recommend I retain/consult an immigration specialist?

Settlement strategy & protections
- What types of settlement provisions should we seek to protect my immigration status and ability to remain/work in the U.S.?
- Are there standard clauses we should include to protect me (e.g., non-waiver of immigration claims, carve-outs for cooperating with immigration proceedings, survivor clauses)?
- Should we negotiate temporary support/maintenance while immigration issues are resolved?
- What confidentiality or nondisparagement language is safe versus risky vis-à-vis testimony or immigration evidence?

Children, custody, and support (if applicable)
- How will custody/visitation be handled temporarily and in a final settlement?
- How is child support calculated here? Can child support or custody terms affect my immigration case?
- What are my parental rights if my immigration status changes?

Financial & property issues
- What immediate financial protections can you get (temporary spousal support, exclusive use of the marital residence, freeze on bank accounts, restraining orders)?
- How will marital assets and debts be divided? What is your view of likely settlement ranges given local law?
- How to protect retirement accounts, pension, joint accounts, and property titles during settlement talks?
- Should we file for temporary orders about property and accounts before settlement discussions?

Evidence & documentation
- What documents should I gather ASAP to support both the divorce case and any immigration claims? (See checklist below.)
- How should I preserve communications and evidence (texts, emails, photos, financial records)?
- Are there any documents I should NOT sign or share with the opposing party without your review?

Safety, harassment, and protection
- If there is past abuse or threats, what immediate protections are available (restraining/protective orders)?
- How will safety concerns be handled during hearings and exchanges (children, visitation)?

Costs, fees & logistics
- What are your fees and costs estimates for trial vs settlement? What costs should I anticipate for immigration counsel, experts, or investigators?
- Do you recommend mediation or settlement conference? If so, when is the best time?
- How will we communicate (email, phone), and what is your expected turnaround on questions?

Settlement negotiation mechanics
- What is our settlement strategy and fallback positions? What is non-negotiable?
- How will offers be documented? Should we use an enforceable written agreement with specific enforcement mechanisms?
- How long after settlement will it take for terms that affect immigration to be finalized and effective?

Post-settlement / enforcement / modification
- How are settlement agreements enforced if the ex-spouse doesn’t comply?
- Can we build in mechanisms for modification if my immigration status changes again, or if financial circumstances change?
- What tax consequences should I consider for any property division, support, or asset transfers?

Evidence & testimony concerns specific to immigration court
- If the divorce settlement includes statements about the nature of the marriage, could that impact the immigration court’s assessment of a bona fide marriage?
- How should I prepare for testimony in immigration court that may overlap with divorce issues?

Practical hearing prep
- What should I bring and prepare for the first hearing?
- Will I be expected to testify at the first hearing? If so, how should I prepare?
- Are there typical forms/motions we should file before the hearing?

Document checklist to prepare (bring copies)
- Government/identity: passports, green card, any visas, I-94, naturalization or application documents
- Immigration filings: copies of petitions, receipts (I-130, I-485, I-751, VAWA, other filings), notices from immigration court
- Marriage certificate and any prior marriage/divorce decrees
- Birth certificates of children
- Financial documents: bank statements (last 12–24 months), pay stubs, tax returns (last 2–3 years), W-2s, 1099s
- Property documents: deeds, mortgage statements, car titles, lease agreements
- Retirement & benefit statements: 401(k), IRA, pension statements
- Debt documents: credit card statements, loan agreements
- Insurance: health, life, homeowners/renters
- Evidence of support/sponsorship forms (I-864 etc.) and any communication about sponsorship
- Communications: relevant texts, emails, social media posts, photos, recordings (if legal in your state)
- Evidence of domestic violence or threats (police reports, photos, medical records)
- Any prenup/postnup or prior agreements
- Any court orders (temporary, protective, immigration court orders)

Practical tips for the meeting
- Prioritize immigration risks and temporary relief first—these can be time-sensitive.
- Bring a short timeline of events (marriage, date of separation, key immigration filings/changes).
- If you have an immigration attorney, arrange a joint call or at least share documents so both attorneys can coordinate.
- Ask Amy to identify three things you must not agree to in a settlement (red lines) and three things you should try to get.

Want me to do any of the following?
- Draft a concise email to Amy Foster with these questions and request a meeting.
- Create a one-page checklist you can print and bring to the hearing.
- Prioritize the top 8–10 questions for a 15-minute calls with Amy.

Which would you like next?

