Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help—below is a focused, practical set of questions and preparation steps you can use with Amy Foster (or any attorney) to protect your interests in divorce settlement talks, plus documents to gather and negotiation tips. If you want, I can turn this into an email to Amy or a printable checklist.

1) Quick clarifying questions for you (so I can tailor further):
- Do you have children with your ex-spouse? (custody/support implications)
- What is your current immigration status (e.g., non‑citizen on visa, lawful permanent resident, pending adjustment, in removal/immigration court)? 
- Is Amy Foster handling both the family/divorce and the immigration matter, or only one of those areas?
- Any history of domestic violence, criminal allegations, or pending criminal charges involving either party?

2) High‑priority questions to ask Amy at your next meeting (bring these up first)
- How will a divorce settlement affect my immigration case and current status? Are there specific settlement terms that could harm my immigration options?
- Are there any statements, admissions, or settlement clauses I should avoid because they could be used in immigration proceedings?
- Do you recommend coordinating settlement negotiations with our immigration strategy? If so, how will you coordinate (with an immigration specialist or court)?
- Should I sign any release, waiver, or confidentiality clause that might limit my ability to pursue immigration relief or to testify in immigration court?
- If my ex‑spouse is/was a sponsor for immigration (e.g., I relied on a spousal petition), what happens to sponsorship or pending petitions if we settle or divorce?
